    <!-- Math annotations -->
    <div class="expl-grid">
      <div class="expl-box blue">
        <h4>S(t) — the fuel curve</h4>
        <p>The dashed blue line shows the susceptible fraction falling monotonically. It starts near 1 (almost everyone susceptible) and declines as people are infected. The epidemic peak occurs exactly when S(t) reaches the threshold \( S^* = N/\mathcal{R}_0 \).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="expl-box orange">
        <h4>I(t) — the epidemic curve</h4>
        <p>The orange bell. It rises while \(\mathcal{R}_e > 1\), peaks when \(\mathcal{R}_e = 1\), then falls. The area under the entire curve equals total person-days of infectiousness. The peak height is determined by \(\mathcal{R}_0\) and the initial susceptible fraction.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="math-block" style="margin-top:.75rem">
      <div class="math-label">The peak condition — derived from dI/dt = 0</div>
      \[ \frac{\mathrm{d}I}{\mathrm{d}t} = \beta \frac{SI}{N} - \gamma I = 0 \implies \frac{\beta S}{N} = \gamma \implies S^* = \frac{N\gamma}{\beta} = \frac{N}{\mathcal{R}_0} \]
      <div class="math-bio">The epidemic peaks when the susceptible pool has been depleted to exactly N/R&#8320;. Neither before nor after — this is a mathematically exact consequence of the SIR equations, not an approximation.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-caption"><strong>Figure 1a.</strong> The epidemic curve I(t) (orange) rises while S(t) &gt; 1/R&#8320;, peaks at the exact moment S = 1/R&#8320; (red dot), then declines. The epidemic ends not because everyone is infected but because S falls below the threshold.</div>
</div>

