    <div class="grid-2">
      <div>
        <div class="math-block">
          <div class="math-label">Solving for S* — set dS/dt = 0 at equilibrium</div>
          \[ \mu N - \frac{\beta S^* I^*}{N} - \mu S^* = 0 \]
          \[ \text{and } \frac{\mathrm{d}I}{\mathrm{d}t} = 0: \quad \frac{\beta S^*}{N} = \gamma + \mu \]
          \[ \therefore \quad S^* = \frac{N(\gamma + \mu)}{\beta} = \frac{N}{\mathcal{R}_0} \]
          <div class="math-bio">S* is the susceptible fraction at which R&#8337; = 1 permanently. Births replenish exactly what infections consume. The same threshold that marks the epidemic peak becomes a fixed point in the endemic model.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="math-block">
          <div class="math-label">Solving for I* — substitute S* into dS/dt = 0</div>
          \[ I^* = \frac{\mu N (N - S^*)}{N \beta S^* / N} = \frac{\mu(\mathcal{R}_0 - 1)}{\beta} \]
          <div class="math-bio">I* is the number of infectious individuals that "burns through" exactly as many susceptibles as births create each day. Exists only when R&#8320; &gt; 1. When R&#8320; = 1, I* = 0 — the borderline case. Larger R&#8320; means higher I* and more circulating disease year-round.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="expl-box orange" style="margin-top:.75rem">
          <h4>What I* means physically</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>I* is the "maintenance level" — constant background infection</li>
            <li>Think: measles in unvaccinated populations before eradication efforts</li>
            <li>I* depends on &#956; (birth rate) — faster population turnover means higher I*</li>
            <li>Vaccination reduces effective R&#8320;, lowering I* toward zero</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <button class="det-btn" onclick="det('spiral')">Why does the endemic system spiral to (S*, I*) rather than going straight there? <span class="di">&#9658;</span></button>
    <div class="det-body" id="spiral">
      <p>Plot S(t) on the x-axis and I(t) on the y-axis, ignoring time — the "phase portrait." The solution traces a clockwise spiral toward (S*, I*). This happens because of the time lag between infection and recovery. When I is large, S depletes rapidly below S* — then the disease decelerates and I falls below I* — then births rebuild S above S* — then the disease accelerates again. Each oscillation is smaller than the last (with &#956; &gt; 0 providing damping) until the system settles at the fixed point. Without demography (&#956; = 0), the spiral is undamped — neutral orbits that never converge. This is why endemic diseases show regular cyclical epidemic waves (measles every 2–3 years, whooping cough every 3–5 years) even in steady state.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="fig-caption"><strong>Figure 4.</strong> S(t) settles at S* = N/R&#8320; (blue) and I(t) settles at I* = &#956;(R&#8320;&#8722;1)/&#946; (orange). Both equilibria are derived by setting the time derivatives to zero simultaneously. I* exists only when R&#8320; &gt; 1.</div>
</div>

