            <td>S(t)</td>
            <td>Susceptibles at time t</td>
            <td>Individuals with no immunity. The "fuel" of the epidemic. Falls as the epidemic progresses; replenished by births (&#956;N) in endemic models. Large S means rapid epidemic growth.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>I(t)</td>
            <td>Infectious at time t</td>
            <td>Currently infected AND transmitting. The epidemic curve is I(t). The area under I(t) = total person-days of infectiousness. Peak of I(t) coincides with S = N/R&#8320;.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>R(t)</td>
            <td>Recovered at time t</td>
            <td>Permanently immune (in SIR). R only increases. R(&#8734;) = total epidemic size. Includes both recovered-immune and disease-induced deaths in the basic SIR formulation.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>N</td>
            <td>S + I + R</td>
            <td>Total population, constant in basic SIR. The /N term in &#946;SI/N scales transmission so that doubling population size does not double R&#8320;.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>&#946;</td>
            <td>Transmission rate (day&#8315;&#185;)</td>
            <td>Contact rate &#215; probability of transmission per contact. Reduced by: masks (&#8595; probability), distancing (&#8595; contacts), isolation (&#8595; contacts of infectious people), quarantine (&#8595; contacts of exposed). Increased by: crowding, indoor settings, high-risk behaviours.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>&#947;</td>
            <td>Recovery rate (day&#8315;&#185;)</td>
            <td>Fraction of I recovering per day. 1/&#947; = mean infectious period in days. Increased by: antiviral treatment, supportive care that shortens illness. Flu: &#947;&#8776;0.2&#8211;0.33. COVID-19: &#947;&#8776;0.1. Measles: &#947;&#8776;0.125.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>&#956;</td>
            <td>Birth/death rate (day&#8315;&#185;)</td>
            <td>Rate of demographic turnover. &#956;N new susceptibles born per day; everyone dies at rate &#956;. For humans: &#956; &#8776; 0.000039/day (life expectancy 70 yr). Critical for endemic modelling; negligible for short outbreaks.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>\(\mathcal{R}_0\)</td>
            <td>&#946;/&#947;</td>
            <td>Average secondary cases from one case in a fully susceptible population. R&#8320; &lt; 1: no epidemic. R&#8320; &gt; 1: epidemic possible. Not a fixed property of the pathogen — context-dependent (crowding, contact patterns, host immunity).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>\(\mathcal{R}_e(t)\)</td>
            <td>R&#8320; &#215; S(t)/N</td>
            <td>Actual secondary cases per case right now. Starts at R&#8320;, falls as S depletes. R&#8337; = 1 at epidemic peak. R&#8337; &lt; 1 means epidemic is declining. Used by public health agencies for real-time outbreak assessment.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>1/R&#8320;</td>
            <td>&#947;/&#946;</td>
            <td>Critical susceptible threshold. Epidemic peaks when S/N = 1/R&#8320;. Endemic S* = 1/R&#8320; permanently. The higher R&#8320;, the lower this threshold — more immunity is needed to stop transmission.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>S*</td>
            <td>(&#947;+&#956;)/&#946; = 1/R&#8320;</td>
            <td>Susceptible fraction at endemic equilibrium. Identical to 1/R&#8320;. Maintained by births topping up what infections consume. Same value as the epidemic threshold — the system is balanced here.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>I*</td>
            <td>&#956;(R&#8320;&#8722;1)/&#946;</td>
            <td>Infectious fraction at endemic equilibrium. The "maintenance level" of disease. Exists only when R&#8320; &gt; 1. Higher R&#8320; and higher &#956; (faster demographic turnover) both increase I*. Vaccination reduces effective R&#8320; and drives I* toward zero.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>v*</td>
            <td>1 &#8722; 1/R&#8320;</td>
            <td>Herd immunity threshold — minimum vaccination coverage to prevent epidemic. Measles (R&#8320;&#8776;15): v* = 93%. COVID-19 original (R&#8320;&#8776;3): v* = 67%. Flu (R&#8320;&#8776;1.3): v* = 23%. Omicron (R&#8320;&#8776;10): v* = 90%.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>dX/dt</td>
            <td>Rate of change of X</td>
            <td>How fast compartment X is changing this instant. dI/dt &gt; 0: epidemic growing. dI/dt = 0: peak. dI/dt &lt; 0: epidemic declining. The sign of dI/dt is entirely determined by whether S &gt; or &lt; N/R&#8320;.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>R(&#8734;)</td>
            <td>Final epidemic size</td>
            <td>Total fraction of population ever infected, at end of epidemic. Implicitly determined by R&#8320;. For R&#8320;=2: R(&#8734;)&#8776;80%. For R&#8320;=5: R(&#8734;)&#8776;99.9%. Always less than 100% (some S survive the epidemic).</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
